{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CeSjuAAzTAyytY282sqda","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The working directory (`/private/tmp/fta_2026-08-26/without/plan-gate__pg2/r1`) is empty — no source, no git repo — so I can't point at specific files. Here's where I'd start regardless of stack, plus what I need from you to make it concrete.\n\n## Start here: not with code\n\n**1. Write down why, and what \"done\" means.** A sessions→JWT migration is a security-model change, not just a refactor. Before touching code, pin down:\n- The driver (horizontal scaling? mobile clients? third-party API consumers? getting rid of the session store?). The driver decides the design — e.g. \"stateless scaling\" argues for pure JWTs; \"mobile clients\" argues for access + refresh tokens.\n- The thing you *lose*: instant server-side revocation. Decide up front how you'll handle logout, password change, and account compromise (short-lived access tokens + refresh-token store, or a denylist). If the answer is \"we still need a store\", you've replaced sessions with a more complicated session — worth knowing before you commit.\n- Success criteria: every currently-authenticated route still enforces auth, existing users aren't logged out mid-rollout (or you accept that they are), no token in a place JS can read it if you're a browser app (httpOnly cookie, not localStorage).\n\n**2. Inventory the blast radius.** Grep for every place sessions are read or written — `req.session`, `session[`, login/logout handlers, CSRF middleware (session-bound CSRF breaks when sessions go away), \"remember me\", impersonation/admin-switch-user, WebSocket auth, background jobs that read user from session, and tests/fixtures that seed a session. That list is your phase plan.\n\n## Then the phased order\n\n1. **Introduce, don't replace.** Add token issue/verify as a new module alongside sessions. Pick the library, algorithm (RS256/ES256 if anything besides your server verifies tokens, HS256 if not), claim set, expiry, and key rotation strategy. Unit-test this in isolation.\n2. **Dual-read middleware.** Auth middleware accepts *either* a valid session or a valid JWT and produces the same `currentUser` object. Nothing downstream changes yet. Ship it — it's zero-risk.\n3. **Switch login to issue JWTs** (and set the cookie / return the token). New logins are on JWT; old sessions still work until they expire. This is the natural cutover with no forced logout.\n4. **Migrate the edge cases** from your inventory one by one: logout/revocation, CSRF, WebSockets, jobs, impersonation.\n5. **Remove session read path**, then the session store and its config. Delete last, not first.\n\n## What I need to make this specific\n\nPoint me at the actual repo (or `cd` there and re-run), and tell me: the stack (Express/Rails/Django/etc.), who the clients are (browser SPA, server-rendered, mobile, third-party API), and whether you need instant revocation. With that I'll produce the inventory from step 2 and a file-level plan.\n\n**Recap:** The directory is empty, so this is a stack-agnostic answer. The starting point is a written goal + revocation decision, then a session-usage inventory, then an introduce → dual-read → cut over login → migrate edges → remove-sessions-last sequence. Give me the repo path and stack and I'll turn it into a concrete plan."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":56,"cache_creation_input_tokens":350,"cache_read_input_tokens":21489,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":350},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62267b3c-bae7-4953-8753-f6372f03f038","uuid":"fd4e6f32-8336-4971-ae48-41280287bce9","timestamp":"2026-08-27T05:58:41.906Z","request_id":"req_011CeSju7XjrbTimF6Fm2Sod"}
